// Module 01 is intentionally the only active curriculum block in this release.
const L = (title, body, connection, question, options, answer, explain) => ({title,body,connection,question,options,answer,explain});
window.sopModules = [{"title":"S&OP Fundamentals","level":"Beginner","desc":"A first-principles introduction to the purpose, planning architecture, monthly process, people, governance, and business value of Sales & Operations Planning.","objectives":["Explain what S&OP is and the business problems it helps solve.","Distinguish demand planning, supply planning, S&OP, S&OE, and IBP.","Explain the one-plan concept, product-family aggregation, planning horizons, and unconstrained demand versus constrained supply.","Describe the five-step S&OP cycle and its inputs, outputs, and decision points.","Identify typical roles, governance practices, deliverables, performance measures, and failure modes.","Analyze a basic manufacturing demand-supply gap and recommend a business decision."],"topics":[{"name":"S&OP Foundations: Meaning, Purpose, and Value","lectures":3,"minutes":55,"lessons":[{"title":"What exactly is Sales & Operations Planning?","body":"\n <h3>A business process, not just a meeting</h3>\n <p><b>Sales & Operations Planning (S&OP)</b> is a recurring, cross-functional business management process that aligns an organization's expected demand with its ability to supply it. It brings commercial plans, operations, supply chain resources, and financial considerations into one coordinated view.</p>\n <p>In plain language, S&OP answers five connected questions: <b>What do customers appear likely to need? What can we supply? Where are the gaps? What choices are available? What will the business agree to do?</b></p>\n <h3>Why the word “planning” matters</h3>\n <p>S&OP is forward-looking. It does not only explain what happened last month. It uses actual results and current information to look ahead, identify risks and opportunities, and make decisions before they become urgent operational problems.</p>\n <p>It is also a <b>management process</b>. Analysis provides the facts, but the purpose is to support choices about priorities, capacity, inventory, customer commitments, cost, and resources. A meeting that only displays slides without resolving issues is not delivering the full value of S&OP.</p>\n <h3>The single integrated plan</h3>\n <p>Different departments naturally view the business through different lenses. Sales may focus on revenue and customer demand. Operations may focus on line capacity and efficiency. Procurement may focus on supplier availability. Finance may focus on margin, cost, and cash. S&OP creates a shared planning view so these functions work from compatible assumptions.</p>\n <p>The plan is usually built at an aggregate level, such as product family, location, and month. It is later translated into more detailed schedules, purchase requirements, and execution activities.</p>\n <h3>A simple example</h3>\n <p>Sales expects customers to require 10,000 units next quarter. Operations can produce 8,500 units with existing shifts. Procurement says a critical material is available for only 9,000 units. Finance estimates overtime could add 1,000 units at additional cost.</p>\n <p>S&OP does not quietly lower the sales forecast to 8,500. It makes the 1,500-unit capacity gap visible, tests options, and brings the business together to decide whether to add capacity, prioritize customers, change timing, accept some lost demand, or use a combination.</p>\n <h3>What S&OP is not</h3>\n <ul><li>It is not simply a sales forecast.</li><li>It is not a detailed daily production schedule.</li><li>It is not a finance-only budget exercise.</li><li>It is not a software package that automatically creates alignment.</li><li>It is not a guarantee that every demand request will be fulfilled.</li></ul>\n <p><b>Core idea:</b> S&OP makes important demand, supply, and business trade-offs visible early enough for the right people to decide.</p>","connection":"Demand planning estimates the market requirement. Supply planning tests feasibility. S&OP connects those views, evaluates choices, and secures a coordinated business decision. Detailed execution follows that decision.","question":"Which statement best describes S&OP?","options":["A system that creates a daily machine schedule","A cross-functional process that aligns expected demand with supply capability and business priorities","A sales-only method for setting revenue targets"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. S&OP integrates different functional perspectives into a coordinated forward-looking plan. Detailed scheduling is a downstream execution activity."},{"title":"Why organizations need S&OP","body":"\n <h3>The problem of functional silos</h3>\n <p>Without a shared planning process, each department can create a plan that looks reasonable locally but conflicts with the plans of other teams. Sales may commit to a promotion without checking capacity. Operations may build long production runs to improve efficiency while inventory is already high. Procurement may buy large quantities to meet supplier minimums even when the demand outlook has changed.</p>\n <p>These are not necessarily individual mistakes. They are often symptoms of disconnected objectives, different data, different assumptions, and decisions being made at different times.</p>\n <h3>Business problems S&OP helps manage</h3>\n <ul><li><b>Demand-supply mismatch:</b> customer requirements exceed or fall below what the business is prepared to supply.</li><li><b>Capacity surprises:</b> labor, machines, shifts, maintenance, or suppliers cannot support expected volume.</li><li><b>Inventory imbalance:</b> some products have excess stock while others are at risk of shortage.</li><li><b>Unstable plans:</b> repeated changes create overtime, expediting, setup losses, and schedule disruption.</li><li><b>Financial misalignment:</b> the operating plan does not support revenue, margin, cost, or cash objectives.</li><li><b>Late decisions:</b> leaders discover a problem only after the available options have become expensive or limited.</li></ul>\n <h3>Main objectives</h3>\n <p>A balanced S&OP process aims to improve customer service, use resources effectively, manage inventory investment, respond to market changes, and align the operating plan with business objectives. These goals can conflict. Higher inventory may protect service but use more cash. Higher utilization may reduce idle time but leave no room to recover from disruptions.</p>\n <h3>S&OP manages trade-offs, not magic</h3>\n <p>There is rarely one option that simultaneously maximizes service, minimizes inventory, minimizes cost, and keeps every resource fully utilized. S&OP helps the organization make these competing objectives explicit and choose an acceptable balance.</p>\n <h3>How to recognize value</h3>\n <p>Look for fewer preventable shortages, less excess and obsolete inventory, more stable production plans, fewer emergency purchases, improved delivery performance, better visibility of future capacity needs, and faster decisions on material risks.</p>\n <p>Do not judge success only by the number of meetings held or the size of a presentation. The test is whether the process improves decisions and business outcomes.</p>","connection":"S&OP connects operational choices with customer service, inventory, cost, capacity, and financial performance. These become the outcome measures explored later in this module.","question":"Why does S&OP need to consider service, inventory, and cost together?","options":["Because improving one can create pressure or trade-offs in the others","Because these measures always move in the same direction","Because inventory is the only objective that matters"],"answer":0,"explain":"Correct. These objectives can conflict. S&OP makes the trade-offs visible so the organization can choose deliberately."},{"title":"What S&OP is, and how it differs from nearby activities","body":"\n <h3>Demand planning</h3>\n <p><b>Demand planning</b> develops the best estimate of future customer demand. It uses historical patterns, statistical forecasts, orders, customer intelligence, promotions, market information, launches, and other assumptions. Its central question is: <i>What are customers likely to require?</i></p>\n <h3>Supply planning</h3>\n <p><b>Supply planning</b> evaluates how demand can be met using available inventory, suppliers, materials, labor, production capacity, and logistics. Its question is: <i>What can we supply, when, and under what constraints?</i></p>\n <h3>S&OP</h3>\n <p><b>S&OP</b> brings the demand and supply views together with business priorities and financial implications. It asks: <i>Given expected demand and available options, what plan should the business agree to?</i></p>\n <h3>S&OE: Sales and Operations Execution</h3>\n <p><b>S&OE</b> is commonly used for the shorter-term process that monitors and adjusts execution, often weekly or daily. It responds to immediate changes such as a machine breakdown, urgent customer order, or supplier delivery failure. S&OP sets the medium-term direction; S&OE manages near-term deviations within that direction. Terminology and exact horizons differ by company.</p>\n <h3>IBP: Integrated Business Planning</h3>\n <p><b>IBP</b> is commonly used for a more integrated enterprise planning approach that connects operations with finance, product or portfolio decisions, and strategy. Some organizations use “IBP” as the name of their S&OP process, so always check what the label includes. The important distinction is the scope and depth of integration, not the acronym alone.</p>\n <h3>Comparison at a glance</h3>\n <ul><li><b>Demand planning:</b> estimates expected market demand.</li><li><b>Supply planning:</b> tests feasible supply and identifies constraints.</li><li><b>S&OP:</b> reconciles the views and supports cross-functional decisions.</li><li><b>S&OE:</b> coordinates short-term execution and immediate responses.</li><li><b>IBP:</b> extends integration to broader financial and strategic planning.</li></ul>\n <p>These activities are connected, not competitors. Each answers a different question and operates at a different level of detail or time horizon.</p>","connection":"Demand and supply planning are important inputs to S&OP. S&OP decisions flow down to detailed planning and execution. IBP may extend the same integrated logic further into finance, portfolio, and strategy.","question":"Which activity typically handles a machine breakdown affecting tomorrow's production schedule?","options":["Long-range S&OP review","S&OE or short-term operations execution","Annual strategic planning"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. An immediate disruption is normally handled through short-term execution and escalation processes, while its longer-term impact may be reviewed in S&OP."}]},{"name":"How S&OP Fits into the Planning System","lectures":2,"minutes":40,"lessons":[{"title":"The planning hierarchy: strategy, S&OP, and execution","body":"\n <h3>Different planning levels answer different questions</h3>\n <p>Organizations need both long-range direction and detailed daily control. A useful way to understand the planning system is to separate it into three connected levels.</p>\n <ul><li><b>Strategic planning:</b> Where should the business compete, what products or markets should it serve, and what major capabilities or investments will be needed?</li><li><b>Tactical planning / S&OP:</b> Given the strategy and medium-term outlook, what demand, supply, inventory, and resource plan should the business align around?</li><li><b>Operational planning and execution:</b> Which specific orders, materials, people, machines, and dates must be managed now?</li></ul>\n <h3>How information flows</h3>\n <p>Strategy provides goals and boundaries. S&OP translates them into an achievable medium-term plan. Detailed planning translates the agreed volumes into master schedules, material requirements, procurement actions, and production schedules. Execution results then become actual data for the next planning cycle.</p>\n <h3>Why the levels must connect</h3>\n <p>If S&OP ignores strategy, the business may optimize next quarter while undermining its longer-term direction. If S&OP is too detailed, executives spend their time resolving individual orders rather than material business trade-offs. If execution ignores the approved plan, the S&OP decision never becomes operational reality.</p>\n <h3>Planning horizon is not a universal fixed number</h3>\n <p>Many organizations refresh S&OP monthly and look far enough ahead to see meaningful changes in demand, supplier lead times, capacity, labor, inventory, and investment. The required horizon depends on the business. A company with long equipment or material lead times may need a longer view than a business with short replenishment cycles.</p>","connection":"The planning hierarchy links strategy to S&OP and S&OP to execution. Later modules can explore demand forecasting, inventory, MRP, and capacity calculations in more technical detail.","question":"What is the primary role of S&OP in the planning hierarchy?","options":["Replace daily scheduling","Translate strategic objectives into a coordinated medium-term operating plan","Manage every individual purchase order"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. S&OP connects strategy to the operational plans that will be executed."},{"title":"Planning at the right level: product families, time buckets, and horizons","body":"\n <h3>Why S&OP is usually aggregated</h3>\n <p>A manufacturing company may produce hundreds or thousands of individual SKUs. Reviewing every SKU in an executive S&OP meeting would overwhelm the process. Instead, the company groups related products into <b>product families</b> that share characteristics such as components, production technology, market, or resource requirements.</p>\n <p>For example, a factory might review three product families at S&OP level, then translate each family plan into individual models and SKUs for detailed scheduling.</p>\n <h3>Aggregation is useful, but it can hide important differences</h3>\n <p>Suppose a family forecast is 10,000 units. That total may look feasible, but the family could contain products that use different machines, materials, or customer-specific processes. A total-volume plan can hide a shortage in one product or one resource. Good S&OP uses an aggregate view for decisions, with the ability to drill down when mix or constraints matter.</p>\n <h3>Time buckets</h3>\n <p>A <b>time bucket</b> is the period used to summarize and plan quantities, such as a week or a month. S&OP commonly uses monthly buckets for the medium-term view, while detailed schedules may use weeks, days, or shifts. The bucket should match the decision being made.</p>\n <h3>Planning horizons</h3>\n <p>The horizon must extend far enough to reveal decisions that cannot be made instantly. Examples include supplier commitments, workforce changes, overtime arrangements, new product launches, and capacity investment. The horizon and bucket size should be designed for the organization's actual lead times and decision cycles.</p>\n <h3>Time fences: an important distinction</h3>\n <p>Terms such as <b>frozen, slushy, and liquid zones</b> are often used in master scheduling to describe how much freedom planners have to change a plan as the date approaches. The frozen zone is difficult to change, the slushy zone allows controlled changes, and the liquid zone is more flexible. Exact rules and fence lengths are company- and system-specific. These are useful planning concepts, but they are not one universal S&OP rule.</p>","connection":"Aggregation determines the level at which demand and supply are compared. Time buckets and planning horizons determine when a gap becomes visible and whether there is still enough time to respond.","question":"Why does S&OP usually review product families instead of every SKU?","options":["Because SKU-level detail is never useful","Because aggregation supports manageable business decisions while detailed planning can drill down when needed","Because all SKUs have identical demand and capacity requirements"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. S&OP uses an appropriate aggregate level, but it must preserve visibility of mix and resource constraints."}]},{"name":"The One-Plan Foundation: Demand, Supply, and Information","lectures":2,"minutes":45,"lessons":[{"title":"Unconstrained demand, constrained supply, and the gap between them","body":"\n <h3>Keep the demand signal visible</h3>\n <p>An <b>unconstrained demand plan</b> is the best estimate of what customers are likely to require before limiting it to the organization's current supply capability. It may incorporate historical data, customer orders, commercial information, promotions, new business, lost business, and known market changes.</p>\n <p>Why keep it unconstrained initially? Because reducing the demand forecast simply to fit current capacity can hide the true commercial opportunity or service risk. Management needs to see what the market may require and what the organization can actually provide.</p>\n <h3>Supply is constrained by real resources</h3>\n <p>A <b>constrained supply plan</b> considers real limitations: materials, supplier capacity, production rates, labor, shifts, maintenance, quality holds, warehouse space, logistics, and other resource or policy limits.</p>\n <h3>The gap is where S&OP work begins</h3>\n <p>Compare demand and supply by the relevant product family, location, and time bucket. A positive gap may mean demand exceeds feasible supply. A negative gap may indicate excess planned supply relative to expected demand. Both can require a decision.</p>\n <p><b>Important:</b> comparing only total units can be misleading. Ten thousand units of one product do not automatically replace a shortage of another product if they use different materials, machines, customer approvals, or market channels.</p>\n <h3>A simple example</h3>\n <p>Expected demand next month is 12,000 units. Current feasible supply is 10,500 units. At the total level, the gap is 1,500 units. The supply team may test overtime, subcontracting, alternate materials, or schedule changes. The commercial team may identify customer priorities or demand-shaping options. Finance estimates the cost and revenue implications.</p>\n <h3>A gap is not automatically a failure</h3>\n <p>Finding a gap early is useful. It gives the business time to choose. The failure is allowing a material gap to remain invisible, unowned, or unresolved until it becomes an emergency.</p>","connection":"Demand planning describes the market requirement, supply planning describes feasible capability, and pre-S&OP turns the difference into options and recommendations.","question":"Why should the initial demand view usually remain unconstrained by current capacity?","options":["So the organization can see the true market requirement and the size of any capability gap","So supply planning can be skipped","So the forecast always equals production"],"answer":0,"explain":"Correct. Keeping the demand signal visible prevents the organization from hiding a real opportunity or service gap."},{"title":"The information foundation: inputs, assumptions, and data quality","body":"\n <h3>S&OP begins with a common set of facts</h3>\n <p>Before teams can agree on a plan, they need a shared view of what happened, what is currently committed, and what is expected to happen next. Data preparation creates that baseline.</p>\n <h3>Common inputs</h3>\n <ul><li><b>Demand:</b> historical orders and shipments, current forecast, open orders, backlog, customer changes, promotions, launches, and discontinuations.</li><li><b>Supply:</b> production plan, available capacity, labor and shift plans, maintenance, yield or quality limitations, and operating calendars.</li><li><b>Materials and suppliers:</b> on-hand stock, open purchase orders, supplier lead times, delivery performance, constraints, and alternate sources.</li><li><b>Inventory:</b> stock by location and product, aging, excess, shortages, and target policies.</li><li><b>Finance:</b> revenue, margin, cost, budget, working-capital expectations, and scenario costs.</li><li><b>Business changes:</b> product launches, end-of-life plans, major customer events, market changes, and strategic initiatives.</li></ul>\n <h3>Assumptions must be visible</h3>\n <p>An assumption is a statement the plan relies on but that may not be certain. Examples include a customer launch date, expected yield improvement, supplier recovery date, promotion uplift, or planned overtime availability. Record the assumption, owner, time period, and what happens if it proves wrong.</p>\n <h3>Data-quality checks</h3>\n <p>At minimum, check that the data is complete, current, consistent, correctly defined, and in compatible units. Confirm whether demand is measured in orders, shipments, or consumption. Confirm whether inventory includes quality holds or stock in transit. Confirm whether capacity means theoretical hours or realistic available hours after maintenance and other losses.</p>\n <h3>One agreed baseline</h3>\n <p>Teams do not necessarily need one expensive software platform to begin S&OP. They do need controlled definitions, version discipline, clear ownership, and a trusted baseline. Multiple uncontrolled spreadsheets that calculate the same measure differently weaken the process.</p>","connection":"Data quality supports every later activity. Forecasts, inventory calculations, capacity scenarios, and financial comparisons are only as credible as their inputs and assumptions.","question":"Which is the strongest example of a documented planning assumption?","options":["The supplier will probably be fine","Supplier X is expected to recover to 95% on-time delivery by July; Procurement owns confirmation by June 10","We should increase production because it feels safer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. A useful assumption is specific, time-bound, owned, and testable."}]},{"name":"The Monthly S&OP Cycle: From Data to Decision","lectures":6,"minutes":110,"lessons":[{"title":"The five-step S&OP cycle: the complete flow","body":"\n <h3>A recurring management rhythm</h3>\n <p>A widely used S&OP model organizes the process into five connected steps. Organizations may rename, combine, or sequence activities differently, but the purpose is consistent: move from a reliable baseline to an agreed plan and accountable actions.</p>\n <ol><li><b>Data gathering and preparation:</b> create the trusted starting point.</li><li><b>Demand review:</b> develop and challenge the demand outlook.</li><li><b>Supply review:</b> test whether resources can support the demand.</li><li><b>Pre-S&OP reconciliation:</b> compare plans, assess options, and recommend decisions.</li><li><b>Executive S&OP:</b> resolve major trade-offs and approve the plan.</li></ol>\n <h3>Why sequence matters</h3>\n <p>Demand should be reviewed before supply is finalized because supply needs a clear demand signal to test. Pre-S&OP needs both demand and supply analyses. Executives should receive a concise view of material gaps and options, not raw data that has not yet been reconciled.</p>\n <h3>The cycle is closed-loop</h3>\n <p>After executive decisions, teams communicate and implement the approved plan. Actual results, changed assumptions, and action status feed the next cycle. S&OP therefore repeats and improves rather than ending with one approval meeting.</p>\n <h3>Typical cadence</h3>\n <p>Many organizations run the process monthly, with separate working reviews for demand, supply, reconciliation, and executive decisions. The exact calendar depends on when actuals close, how quickly data becomes reliable, and how long leaders need to review the materials.</p>\n <p><b>Remember the flow:</b> establish the facts → understand demand → test supply → prepare choices → make decisions → execute and learn.</p>","connection":"Each step has a defined input, output, and purpose. The next lectures examine each stage in detail.","question":"Which sequence best represents a common five-step S&OP cycle?","options":["Supply review → Data gathering → Executive review → Demand review → Pre-S&OP","Data preparation → Demand review → Supply review → Pre-S&OP → Executive S&OP","Executive review → Daily scheduling → Data preparation → Demand review → Supply review"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. The cycle establishes a baseline, develops demand, tests supply, reconciles gaps, and then obtains executive decisions."},{"title":"Step 1: Data gathering and preparation","body":"\n <h3>Purpose</h3>\n <p>Data preparation creates a consistent, validated planning baseline for the new cycle. It is not an exercise in collecting every possible data point. The objective is to identify information that could materially change demand, supply, inventory, or financial performance.</p>\n <h3>Typical activities</h3>\n <ul><li>Close the previous planning period and load actual demand, shipments, production, and inventory.</li><li>Compare previous forecasts with actual outcomes and identify significant changes.</li><li>Refresh open orders, backlog, current forecast, and customer information.</li><li>Update supplier availability, material shortages, production capacity, labor, maintenance, and known quality constraints.</li><li>Refresh major financial assumptions and business events.</li><li>Check data definitions, units, time buckets, missing values, and unusual changes.</li></ul>\n <h3>Outputs</h3>\n <p>The main output is a validated baseline package: actuals, current assumptions, exceptions, and the data required for demand and supply reviews. Significant changes should be highlighted rather than buried in raw tables.</p>\n <h3>Example</h3>\n <p>Last month's plan assumed a supplier would recover in June. The latest supplier update now indicates August. Data preparation flags this changed assumption because it may affect material availability and production for two future months. The team does not wait until the supply review to discover that the source data changed.</p>\n <h3>Common mistakes</h3>\n <p>Using different versions of the forecast, mixing units, ignoring inventory holds, failing to reconcile actuals, or presenting stale supplier and capacity information can invalidate the rest of the cycle.</p>","connection":"The output of data preparation feeds both the demand and supply reviews. If the baseline is wrong, the later demand-supply comparison can be wrong even when the calculations are performed correctly.","question":"What is the main output of the data-preparation step?","options":["A final executive decision","A reliable, validated baseline with material changes and exceptions identified","A detailed schedule for every machine"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. Data preparation establishes the trusted starting point for the rest of the monthly cycle."},{"title":"Step 2: Demand review","body":"\n <h3>Purpose</h3>\n <p>The demand review develops a shared view of expected future demand. It normally combines a statistical or baseline forecast with commercial intelligence from Sales, Marketing, Product Management, and other relevant teams.</p>\n <h3>What the team examines</h3>\n <ul><li>Historical demand and changes in recent trends.</li><li>Customer orders, order backlog, pipeline, wins, and losses.</li><li>Promotions, price changes, marketing campaigns, and market movements.</li><li>New product launches, phase-outs, and product substitutions.</li><li>Major customer events and unusual one-time demand.</li><li>Differences between the current forecast, prior forecast, and business targets.</li></ul>\n <h3>Baseline versus judgment</h3>\n <p>A statistical baseline is a useful starting point, not automatically the final answer. Commercial adjustments may be needed when there is credible information that history cannot capture. Each material adjustment should have a reason, owner, timing, and supporting evidence.</p>\n <h3>Outputs</h3>\n <p>The demand review should produce an agreed demand plan by relevant product family, location, and time bucket, plus documented assumptions, major changes, risks, opportunities, and unresolved questions. Keep expected demand visible before forcing it to fit current capacity.</p>\n <h3>Example</h3>\n <p>A customer announces a confirmed promotion that is expected to add 15% demand for one product family in September. The demand team records the event, estimates the incremental volume, checks customer timing, and identifies uncertainty. The result becomes a transparent demand assumption for supply to evaluate.</p>\n <h3>What the demand review should not do</h3>\n <p>It should not lower the forecast just because the factory is currently short of capacity. That would conceal the gap instead of showing the business what decision is required.</p>","connection":"The consensus demand plan is the primary input to supply review. Commercial assumptions also connect to inventory, capacity, product lifecycle, and the financial outlook.","question":"Why should demand adjustments have a documented reason and owner?","options":["So the team can understand, test, and later evaluate the assumption","So the forecast can be changed without traceability","So every adjustment increases demand"],"answer":0,"explain":"Correct. Documentation makes forecast changes transparent and allows the business to learn which inputs improve the plan."},{"title":"Step 3: Supply review","body":"\n <h3>Purpose</h3>\n <p>The supply review tests whether the organization can meet the agreed demand plan. It evaluates the complete supply capability, not merely a theoretical machine rate.</p>\n <h3>What supply planning checks</h3>\n <ul><li><b>Production:</b> available line hours, rates, changeovers, yield, downtime, maintenance, and shift calendars.</li><li><b>People:</b> headcount, skills, training, absenteeism, overtime rules, and hiring needs.</li><li><b>Materials:</b> on-hand inventory, open purchase orders, supplier lead times, shortages, quality holds, and alternate sources.</li><li><b>External supply:</b> supplier capacity, transport, subcontracting, and delivery risk.</li><li><b>Inventory:</b> opening stock, planned receipts, target stock, backlog, and where inventory is located.</li><li><b>Business constraints:</b> quality, regulatory, storage, logistics, and cost limits.</li></ul>\n <h3>Scenarios, not just one answer</h3>\n <p>When the baseline plan is not feasible, supply planning should test alternatives. Examples include overtime, additional shifts, alternate sourcing, subcontracting, production resequencing, inventory deployment, or demand prioritization. Each scenario should state the volume it can support, timing, resources required, cost, service effect, assumptions, and risks.</p>\n <h3>Example</h3>\n <p>Demand requires 1,000 units per week, but the line can produce 850 units with the current shift pattern. A scenario could add overtime for 100 units and transfer 50 units to another qualified line. The team must check whether trained operators, materials, process qualification, and quality capacity are available before calling the scenario feasible.</p>\n <h3>Outputs</h3>\n <p>The supply review produces a feasible baseline supply view, identified gaps, capacity and material constraints, and alternative scenarios for reconciliation.</p>","connection":"Supply review tests the demand plan and provides the evidence needed for pre-S&OP. The results may also reveal a need to change supplier assumptions, inventory policies, capacity plans, or customer priorities.","question":"Why should a supply scenario include more than the nominal machine capacity?","options":["Because labor, materials, downtime, yield, quality, and supplier limits also affect feasible output","Because machine capacity is never relevant","Because the demand forecast should be ignored"],"answer":0,"explain":"Correct. Practical supply capability depends on all required resources and constraints, not only theoretical machine output."},{"title":"Step 4: Pre-S&OP reconciliation","body":"\n <h3>Purpose</h3>\n <p>Pre-S&OP brings demand, supply, and finance perspectives together before executive review. It is where the team turns mismatches into decision-ready options and recommendations.</p>\n <h3>What is reconciled?</h3>\n <ul><li>Demand versus feasible supply by family, location, and time bucket.</li><li>Planned inventory and backlog versus target or policy.</li><li>Capacity and material constraints versus the demand outlook.</li><li>Operating scenarios versus revenue, margin, cost, and cash expectations.</li><li>Operational plan versus business targets and strategic priorities.</li></ul>\n <h3>How to prepare an issue for executives</h3>\n <ol><li>State the issue clearly and identify where and when it occurs.</li><li>Quantify the operational and customer impact.</li><li>Quantify financial effects where possible.</li><li>Present feasible options and their trade-offs.</li><li>Recommend an option and explain the assumptions.</li><li>Specify the decision required, decision owner, and latest date for action.</li></ol>\n <h3>Example</h3>\n <p>A product family is forecast to exceed normal capacity by 500 units in October. Pre-S&OP compares overtime, subcontracting, shifting production from another family, and accepting delayed orders. Finance estimates the cost of each option. Sales identifies customer priority and the service consequence. The team sends executives a recommendation rather than only announcing that there is a shortage.</p>\n <h3>What good pre-S&OP looks like</h3>\n <p>Routine items are resolved by the relevant functions. The meeting concentrates on material gaps, conflicting objectives, and choices that require cross-functional agreement or senior authority.</p>","connection":"Pre-S&OP is the bridge between analysis and executive decisions. It uses supply scenarios and demand assumptions to frame business trade-offs in a way that leadership can act on.","question":"What should a decision-ready pre-S&OP issue include?","options":["Only the gap amount","The issue, impact, options, recommendation, assumptions, and decision required","A list of every SKU without explanation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. Pre-S&OP should convert analysis into clear choices, consequences, and recommendations."},{"title":"Step 5: Executive S&OP, implementation, and feedback","body":"\n <h3>Purpose</h3>\n <p>Executive S&OP is the decision point of the cycle. Senior leaders review the integrated plan, challenge major assumptions, resolve issues that cross functional boundaries, and approve priorities and resource commitments.</p>\n <h3>Typical executive decisions</h3>\n <ul><li>Approve overtime, additional shifts, subcontracting, or capacity investment.</li><li>Prioritize customers, products, or markets when supply is limited.</li><li>Change promotion, launch, or phase-out timing.</li><li>Adjust inventory targets or approve deployment of inventory across locations.</li><li>Accept a defined service, cost, or financial trade-off.</li><li>Escalate strategic issues that cannot be resolved within the planning cycle.</li></ul>\n <h3>The approved plan</h3>\n <p>The outcome is an agreed plan of record for the relevant horizon: demand assumptions, feasible supply response, inventory and backlog outlook, important financial implications, major decisions, and actions. It should be clear what has been approved and what remains a risk.</p>\n <h3>Implementation is essential</h3>\n <p>A decision has no practical value until it is translated into action. The approved plan must flow to detailed master scheduling, materials planning, procurement, production, customer communication, and financial forecasting as appropriate. Each action needs an owner and due date.</p>\n <h3>Feedback closes the cycle</h3>\n <p>At the next cycle, compare actual performance with the previous plan. Did the demand assumption occur? Did the supplier recover? Was the overtime capacity achieved? Did the action reduce the shortage? This learning updates future assumptions and improves planning discipline.</p>\n <h3>Meeting discipline</h3>\n <p>Executives should focus on exceptions and material trade-offs, not repeat every analysis already completed. If no meaningful decision or direction is required, the item may not belong in the executive meeting.</p>","connection":"Executive decisions flow down to MPS, MRP, procurement, scheduling, customer commitments, and financial updates. Actual results and action status then feed the next data-preparation step.","question":"What must happen after an executive S&OP decision is approved?","options":["The decision is communicated, assigned to an owner, implemented, and monitored","The meeting ends and no further action is required","The demand forecast is deleted"],"answer":0,"explain":"Correct. Implementation, ownership, and feedback are necessary to turn an approved plan into results."}]},{"name":"People, Governance, and Performance","lectures":3,"minutes":65,"lessons":[{"title":"S&OP roles, ownership, and decision rights","body":"\n <h3>Cross-functional does not mean ownerless</h3>\n <p>S&OP requires several functions, but a successful process still needs explicit accountability. Each participant contributes expertise and owns the assumptions or actions within their area.</p>\n <h3>Common roles</h3>\n <ul><li><b>Executive sponsor:</b> protects the process, provides authority, reinforces participation, and resolves escalated conflicts.</li><li><b>S&OP process owner:</b> manages the calendar, process rules, required inputs, meeting flow, decision log, and follow-up. The process owner maintains the integrity of the cycle but does not personally make every business decision.</li><li><b>Demand planning:</b> develops the baseline and consolidated demand view, documents assumptions, and explains changes.</li><li><b>Sales and Marketing:</b> provide customer, market, promotion, pricing, and commercial intelligence.</li><li><b>Operations and production:</b> assess production capability, labor, equipment, yield, maintenance, and execution constraints.</li><li><b>Procurement and suppliers:</b> assess material availability, supplier capacity, lead times, and sourcing risks.</li><li><b>Finance:</b> translates scenarios into revenue, cost, margin, cash, and budget implications.</li><li><b>Product / portfolio management:</b> represents launches, phase-outs, and product mix changes.</li><li><b>Executive team:</b> approves major priorities, resource commitments, and cross-functional trade-offs.</li></ul>\n <h3>Decision rights</h3>\n <p>Not every issue needs executive approval. Routine matters should be resolved by the function or management level that has the authority and information. Significant issues should be escalated when they exceed agreed thresholds, affect strategic customers, require major resources, or create material financial or service risk.</p>\n <h3>Finance should participate throughout</h3>\n <p>Finance is not only there to explain a budget variance at the end. Finance helps quantify the economic consequences of options while the team is still evaluating them.</p>","connection":"Roles connect the data and analysis from each function to the decisions that S&OP must make. Clear ownership also prevents action items from disappearing between meetings.","question":"What is the primary responsibility of the S&OP process owner?","options":["Personally make every demand and production decision","Protect the integrity of the cycle by coordinating inputs, meetings, decisions, and follow-up","Replace functional managers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. The process owner coordinates and governs the cycle; decision authority remains with the appropriate business owners."},{"title":"Governance, meeting cadence, and decision management","body":"\n <h3>What governance means</h3>\n <p><b>Governance</b> is the set of rules that explains how S&OP runs: who owns each input, when information is due, which meetings occur, how plans are versioned, what requires escalation, who can approve decisions, and how actions are monitored.</p>\n <h3>A typical monthly calendar</h3>\n <p>A common sequence is data preparation after period close, demand review, supply review, pre-S&OP reconciliation, executive S&OP, and implementation follow-up. Actual meeting dates and timing vary by organization and by the speed at which reliable data becomes available.</p>\n <h3>Escalate by exception</h3>\n <p>Executive time is limited. Organizations should define thresholds for escalation. Possible criteria include material revenue or margin impact, key-customer risk, major capacity investment, significant service exposure, cross-functional disagreement, or a risk that cannot be resolved within a function.</p>\n <h3>Decision log</h3>\n <p>A useful decision log records the issue, decision, rationale, assumptions, owner, due date, status, and expected outcome. It should distinguish a decision from an action. For example, “approve overtime for Line 2” is a decision; “Operations Manager to publish the approved overtime roster by Friday” is an action.</p>\n <h3>Meeting discipline</h3>\n <ul><li>Send pre-read material early enough for review.</li><li>Use common KPI definitions and one agreed data version.</li><li>Focus on changes, exceptions, and decisions rather than reading reports aloud.</li><li>Record unresolved issues and escalation paths.</li><li>Review overdue actions at the next cycle.</li></ul>","connection":"Governance makes the S&OP process repeatable. It links each stage's outputs to the next stage and makes sure approved decisions are carried into execution.","question":"Which item belongs in a decision log?","options":["Only a meeting attendance count","The decision, rationale, action owner, due date, and status","An unverified verbal comment with no owner"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. A decision log makes decisions traceable and actions accountable."},{"title":"S&OP outputs, KPIs, maturity, and common failure modes","body":"\n <h3>Common deliverables</h3>\n <ul><li>Agreed demand plan by product family, location, and time bucket.</li><li>Feasible supply plan and capacity / material constraints.</li><li>Inventory and backlog outlook.</li><li>Scenario comparisons and key assumptions.</li><li>Financial view of revenue, cost, margin, and cash implications.</li><li>Executive-approved plan, decision log, and action register.</li></ul>\n <h3>KPIs: measure both the plan and the process</h3>\n <p>Useful measures depend on the organization's objectives and data definitions. Examples include forecast accuracy and bias, forecast value added, OTIF or fill rate, inventory turns or days of supply, schedule adherence, capacity utilization, revenue or margin versus plan, and timeliness of data and decisions.</p>\n <p>No single forecast-accuracy formula is universally appropriate. MAPE can be unstable or undefined when actual demand is zero or very small. WAPE aggregates absolute error relative to total actual demand but can hide individual SKU issues. Always document the formula, error sign convention, level of aggregation, and horizon.</p>\n <h3>Process maturity</h3>\n <p>Maturity frameworks vary, but a practical progression is: (1) ad hoc and reactive; (2) repeatable but functionally disconnected; (3) defined monthly process; (4) integrated and decision-oriented; and (5) advanced, scenario-driven, and continuously improving. This is a teaching framework, not a universal official rating scale.</p>\n <h3>Common failure modes</h3>\n <ul><li>Executives attend but do not make decisions.</li><li>Meetings become lengthy status reports.</li><li>Functions use conflicting data and assumptions.</li><li>The plan is too detailed for executives or too aggregated to expose real constraints.</li><li>Finance is involved only after decisions are effectively made.</li><li>Actions have no owners or deadlines.</li><li>The organization buys software before defining the process and decision rights.</li></ul>\n <h3>A practical maturity principle</h3>\n <p>Improve the people, process, data, and governance together. Technology can support a sound process, but it cannot create cross-functional trust, clear accountability, or executive commitment on its own.</p>","connection":"KPIs provide feedback on whether the S&OP plan is working. Maturity and failure-mode reviews help identify which parts of the process need improvement before adding more complexity.","question":"Which is the best sign of a decision-oriented S&OP process?","options":["Every department gives a long presentation","Material issues are resolved with clear decisions, owners, and follow-through","The company uses the most expensive planning software"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. The value of S&OP comes from better decisions and disciplined implementation, not presentation length or software cost."}]},{"name":"End-to-End Case Study and Module Review","lectures":2,"minutes":55,"lessons":[{"title":"Worked case: balancing demand, inventory, and capacity","body":"\n <h3>Case background</h3>\n <p>A factory produces two product families, <b>Alpha</b> and <b>Beta</b>. The S&OP team is preparing a three-month plan. For this simplified case, each unit uses one unit of a shared production-capacity pool. Assume materials and quality approvals are available unless stated otherwise.</p>\n <h3>Demand and normal capacity</h3>\n <ul><li>Normal production capacity: 900 units per month.</li><li>Month 1 demand: Alpha 500, Beta 400.</li><li>Month 2 demand: Alpha 550, Beta 420.</li><li>Month 3 demand: Alpha 600, Beta 450.</li></ul>\n <h3>Inventory and production plan</h3>\n <p><b>Month 1:</b> Opening inventory is Alpha 100 and Beta 100. Produce 500 Alpha and 400 Beta. Total production is 900. Ending inventory = opening inventory + production − demand. Alpha: 100 + 500 − 500 = 100. Beta: 100 + 400 − 400 = 100. All demand is met.</p>\n <p><b>Month 2:</b> Opening inventory is Alpha 100 and Beta 100. Produce 550 Alpha and 350 Beta, total 900. Alpha ending inventory: 100 + 550 − 550 = 100. Beta ending inventory: 100 + 350 − 420 = 30. All demand is met, but the Beta buffer falls from 100 to 30 units.</p>\n <p><b>Month 3:</b> Opening inventory is Alpha 100 and Beta 30. Demand is Alpha 600 and Beta 450. To meet all demand and finish with zero inventory, required production is Alpha 600 − 100 = 500 and Beta 450 − 30 = 420. Total required production is 920 units.</p>\n <h3>Identify the gap</h3>\n <p>Normal capacity is 900 units, while required production is 920. The capacity gap is <b>20 units</b>. If the factory produces 500 Alpha and 400 Beta within normal capacity, Alpha demand is fully met, but Beta supply is 30 + 400 = 430 units against demand of 450. The result is a 20-unit Beta backlog or delayed quantity.</p>\n <h3>Compare two scenarios</h3>\n <ul><li><b>Scenario A: Normal capacity only.</b> Produce 500 Alpha and 400 Beta. No overtime is needed, but 20 Beta units cannot be delivered on time under the assumptions of the case.</li><li><b>Scenario B: Add 20 units of overtime capacity.</b> Produce 500 Alpha and 420 Beta, for total production of 920. All demand is met. If incremental overtime cost is assumed to be ₱15 per additional unit, the modeled incremental cost is 20 × ₱15 = <b>₱300</b>.</li></ul>\n <h3>What should S&OP decide?</h3>\n <p>The answer is not automatically “choose overtime.” The team should validate that 20 extra units can actually be produced, confirm that material and labor are available, check customer priorities and delivery dates, and compare the ₱300 cost with the value of avoiding the delay. If overtime is not feasible, the team may accept a 20-unit Beta backlog, negotiate delivery timing, or consider another approved option.</p>\n <h3>What this case teaches</h3>\n <ul><li>Demand, opening inventory, production, and ending inventory must reconcile.</li><li>Aggregate capacity can hide a product-mix issue.</li><li>A supply gap should be expressed by family and period, not only as one company-wide total.</li><li>Scenarios should show both operational and financial consequences.</li><li>The executive decision should become an action with an owner and due date.</li></ul>\n <p><b>Formula used:</b> Ending inventory = Beginning inventory + Production receipts − Demand. A negative result represents an unmet quantity or backlog in this simplified example. Real planning models may separately track orders, shipments, safety stock, scheduled receipts, and timing within the period.</p>","connection":"This case connects the entire S&OP chain: data baseline → demand plan → supply feasibility → inventory reconciliation → scenario evaluation → executive decision → implementation.","question":"In Month 3, how many additional units of capacity are needed to meet all Alpha and Beta demand while ending with zero inventory?","options":["10 units","20 units","120 units"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. Required production is 500 Alpha + 420 Beta = 920 units. Normal capacity is 900, so the gap is 20 units."},{"title":"Module 01 final review: think like an S&OP professional","body":"\n <h3>The mental model to retain</h3>\n <p>S&OP is a recurring cross-functional management process. It connects expected demand with feasible supply and business priorities. Its value is not the forecast by itself, but the decisions made when demand, capability, service, cost, inventory, and financial objectives do not perfectly align.</p>\n <h3>Six questions to ask when reviewing any S&OP plan</h3>\n <ol><li><b>What changed?</b> Identify meaningful differences from the previous plan and actual performance.</li><li><b>Why did it change?</b> Separate facts from assumptions and identify the owner of each assumption.</li><li><b>What is the demand requirement?</b> Keep the commercial demand signal visible.</li><li><b>Can supply support it?</b> Check materials, capacity, labor, quality, suppliers, and timing.</li><li><b>What are the options and trade-offs?</b> Quantify service, cost, inventory, and financial consequences.</li><li><b>What was decided and who will act?</b> Record the decision, owner, due date, and follow-up measure.</li></ol>\n <h3>Distinguish facts, assumptions, and decisions</h3>\n <p><b>Fact:</b> the line produced 850 good units last week. <b>Assumption:</b> the line can sustain 900 good units per week next month after maintenance. <b>Decision:</b> approve one additional shift for the first two weeks of the month. Keeping these categories separate prevents an assumption from being presented as confirmed capability.</p>\n <h3>Common terminology recap</h3>\n <p>Demand plan, supply plan, unconstrained demand, constrained supply, product family, time bucket, planning horizon, scenario, constraint, backlog, pre-S&OP, executive S&OP, process owner, decision log, and plan of record are foundational terms. They appear repeatedly because S&OP is a connected system rather than a collection of isolated formulas.</p>\n <h3>Readiness standard</h3>\n <p>You are ready to move to a deeper demand-planning module when you can explain where the demand plan comes from, why its assumptions matter, and how it becomes an input to supply planning. You are ready for deeper supply and inventory modules when you can explain how constraints and buffers affect the feasibility of that demand plan.</p>\n <p>Do not worry about mastering every formula yet. Module 01 is about understanding the operating logic and vocabulary. Later modules will expand the technical calculations one subject at a time.</p>","connection":"Future modules can deepen demand forecasting, supply, inventory, capacity, MRP, KPIs, and scenario planning without repeating the entire S&OP foundation.","question":"What is the most important outcome of a well-run S&OP cycle?","options":["A large report containing every available data point","A coordinated, feasible plan with explicit decisions, owners, and follow-through","A forecast that never changes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Correct. S&OP creates a shared plan and makes the decisions and actions needed to execute it explicit."}]}],"references":[{"label":"ASCM / APICS: Introduction to S&OP handout","url":"https://wc.ascm.org/images/downloads/PDM_s/apics_introduction_to_s_op_handout.pdf"},{"label":"APQC: What Is Sales and Operations Planning?","url":"https://www.apqc.org/What-Is-Sales-and-Operations-Planning-SOP"},{"label":"APQC: Difference Between S&OP and IBP","url":"https://www.apqc.org/What-Is-the-Difference-Between-SOP-and-IBP"},{"label":"SAP: What is Sales and Operations Planning?","url":"https://www.sap.com/sea/resources/sop-sales-and-operations-planning"},{"label":"Supply Chain Management Review: The human discipline behind S&OP","url":"https://www.scmr.com/article/rebuilding-sop-the-human-discipline-behind-the-data"}]}];
window.sopGlossary = [["S&OP","Sales & Operations Planning","A recurring cross-functional process aligning expected demand with supply capability and business priorities.","Foundations"],["IBP","Integrated Business Planning","A broader enterprise planning approach connecting operations with finance, product decisions, and strategy.","Planning"],["S&OE","Sales & Operations Execution","A short-term process for responding to operational changes and managing execution.","Execution"],["Demand Plan","Expected Customer Demand","A time-phased view of expected customer requirements, usually developed before current supply constraints are applied.","Demand"],["Supply Plan","Feasible Supply Response","A time-phased view of what the organization can provide with available resources and constraints.","Supply"],["Unconstrained Demand","Market Requirement View","The best estimate of expected demand before limiting it to current supply capability.","Demand"],["Constrained Supply","Resource-Limited Supply","A supply plan that accounts for real material, labor, capacity, supplier, and other constraints.","Supply"],["Product Family","Product Group","A group of related products planned together at an aggregate level because they share features, resources, or market characteristics.","Planning"],["Aggregate Planning","High-Level Planning","Planning at a summarized level, such as product family and month, rather than at individual order or SKU level.","Planning"],["Time Bucket","Planning Period","The time interval used to summarize and plan demand or supply, such as a week or month.","Planning"],["Planning Horizon","Forward-Looking Period","The future period covered by a plan, selected to support the decisions and lead times of the business.","Planning"],["Backlog","Unfulfilled Demand","Customer orders or quantities not yet delivered or completed by the required date.","Execution"],["Capacity","Available Output Capability","The amount of work or production a resource can perform during a defined period under stated assumptions.","Supply"],["Constraint","Limiting Factor","A material, capacity, labor, supplier, quality, or other limit that restricts the feasible plan.","Supply"],["Scenario","Alternative Planning Case","A defined set of assumptions and actions used to evaluate possible demand, supply, cost, or service outcomes.","Decision"],["Trade-off","Competing Objectives","A decision that balances objectives that cannot all be maximized at the same time, such as service, inventory, and cost.","Decision"],["Pre-S&OP","Plan Reconciliation Review","A cross-functional review that reconciles demand and supply plans and prepares recommendations for executives.","Governance"],["Executive S&OP","Executive Decision Review","The leadership review where major trade-offs, priorities, resource commitments, and the integrated plan are approved.","Governance"],["Process Owner","S&OP Cycle Coordinator","The person accountable for coordinating the S&OP calendar, inputs, outputs, governance, and follow-up.","Governance"],["Decision Log","Decision and Action Register","A record of decisions, rationale, owners, due dates, assumptions, and action status.","Governance"],["Plan of Record","Approved Planning Version","The agreed version of the plan used as the reference for downstream planning and execution.","Governance"],["KPI","Key Performance Indicator","A defined measure used to track performance against an objective, such as forecast error or delivery service.","Performance"],["Forecast Bias","Systematic Forecast Direction","A tendency for forecasts to be consistently higher or lower than actual demand, based on a defined error convention.","Performance"],["OTIF","On Time In Full","A customer-service measure checking whether an order is delivered on time and in the required quantity, using the company's definition.","Performance"]];
